Repositories typically read `response.data` and (when appropriate) hydrate
with a domain class.

## Tooling

- **Context7 MCP** — use it for any library, framework, SDK, or API
  documentation, setup, configuration, or CLI usage. Don't wait to be asked.
  If Context7 MCP is not available in the current session, tell the user
  before falling back to other sources.

## Basic Project Commands

```bash
npm install                                # install deps (Node version is fixed — do not bump)
npm run dev                                # Vite dev server (default API mode)
npm run dev:local                          # VITE_API_MODE=local
npm run dev:remote                         # VITE_API_MODE=remote
npm run build                              # type-check + production build
npm run type-check                         # vue-tsc only
npm run test                               # vitest --run (full suite)
npm run test:watch                         # vitest watch mode
npm run test:coverage                      # vitest coverage + open report
npm run lint:check                         # ESLint, no fixes
npm run lint:fix                           # ESLint with --fix
npm run format                             # Prettier write on src/
```

Runtime configuration is loaded from `<config source>` at boot
(e.g. `public/config.json` fetched by `src/utils/config.ts`) — `API_URL`,
MSAL `CLIENT_ID` / `TENANT_ID` / `BACKEND_CLIENT_ID`, base path, App Insights.
The CI variant lives at `public/config.ci.json`. Do not hard-code these
values in source.

## Testing

- **Vitest 3** with **jsdom** is the runner. Test files live next to the
  code in `__tests__/` folders (matched by the ESLint Vitest config).
- **Render through the test harness, not bare `render()`.** Use
  `renderWithProviders` from `src/test-utils/render.ts` — it wires Pinia,
  Vue Query (with retry off + Infinity cache), `vue-router-mock`, and the
  `ApToast` / `ApModal` / `ErrorProvider` providers needed by the app.
  Custom stubs for those providers will diverge from production behavior.
- For repository / composable tests, mock `useHttpClient` rather than
  `axios` so the auth + error pipeline contract stays explicit.

## Code Conventions

- **Composition API only** in new code. Use `<script setup lang="ts">` for
  SFCs. Options API additions, `Vue.set`, and Vue 2 filters are not allowed.
- **Define-macros order** is enforced by ESLint: `defineProps` → `defineEmits`
  → `defineModel`. Props and emits must use the type-based declaration.
- **Import order** is enforced (`builtin → external → internal → parent →
  sibling → index`, newline between groups, alphabetized). `vue` first
  within `external`, then `@<org>/*`, then `eslint/*`; `@/**` is `internal`.
- **State:** app-wide state lives in **Pinia** stores (`src/stores/` —
  `auth`, `errorStore`, `featureFlags` — and per-module `stores/`). Don't
  store app state in module-scoped `let` / `ref` outside a Pinia store.
- **Errors:** throw the typed errors from `src/models/api-errors`
  (`BadRequest`, `Forbidden`, `NotFound`, `Timeout`, `Unauthorized`,
  `NetworkError`, `Unknown`). The default error UX is the global
  `ErrorProvider` + `errorStore` modal; opt a request out with `axios`
  config `skipErrorModal: true` only when the caller handles the error
  inline.
- **Routes:** each module exposes a `routes.ts` array merged into the router
  by `src/router/index.ts`. Use route `meta` for `pageTitle`, `featureFlag`
  (key of `featureFlags` store), and `requiredAccessLevels` (array of
  `AccessLevel`). The `mainGuard` enforces both — don't bypass it.
- **Code splitting:** route components are loaded with dynamic `import()`
  (see any module's `routes.ts`). Keep new routes the same way.
- **Prettier** is the formatter: single quotes, semicolons, 2 spaces,
  trailing comma `es5`, print width **140**, one attribute per line. Run
  `npm run format` before committing.
- **Comments:** the agent honors the minimal-comments rule
  ([`asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md` → Rule #6](../asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md)).
  Inline comments only when genuinely necessary; file-header of ≤3 lines
  is the only agent-initiated comment allowed.

## Commit & PR Conventions

- Commit messages are checked in CI (e.g. by `scripts/commit-check.sh`).
  Each commit in the PR must match:

  ```text
  <type>(<scope>): <lowercase description> #<TICKET-ID>
  ```

  - **Allowed types:** `feat | fix | docs | style | refactor | test | chore
    | perf | ci | build | revert`.
  - Description starts **lowercase** after the colon.
  - Message **must end with `#<TICKET-ID>`** (e.g. `#1234`).
- **Base branch:** `development` (or per `base_branch` in the frontmatter).
- **Branch name convention:** `feature/<TICKET-ID>-<short-slug>` (or
  `fix/…`, `chore/…`).
- **One ticket → one focused PR.**

## What NOT to Do

- **Do not bump the Node.js version.** This is an explicit project rule.
- **Do not call `axios` / `fetch` directly.** Always go through
  `useHttpClient` so MSAL token injection, 401 retry, and the error pipeline
  run.
- **Do not import with relative parent paths (`../`)** — ESLint blocks it.
  Use `@/`.
- **Do not introduce Options API, `Vue.set`, or Vue 2 filters** in new code.
- **Do not bypass `mainGuard`** by removing `meta.featureFlag` /
  `meta.requiredAccessLevels` to land a feature; gate it properly or
  escalate.
- **Do not store app state outside Pinia** (no module-level mutable
  singletons for shared state).
- **Do not add inline comments** to generated code except when genuinely
  necessary, when they carry important non-obvious context, or when the
  user explicitly asked. Only a **file-header block of at most 3 lines**
  may be added on the agent's initiative. See
  [`asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md` → Rule #6](../asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md)
  for the full rule; do not restate it here.
- **Do not run `git push --force` on shared branches** and **do not commit
  with `--no-verify`**.
- **Do not skip the commit message format** — CI will reject the PR.
